        <h2>Music Streaming Hacks</h2>
        <p>In my first year of high school, I spent several months building a program that downloaded paid music from Chinese streaming services without authorisation. I did not understand the legal and ethical implications clearly when I started. Once I did, I shut the service down. I am not proud of the infringement, but the project remains part of my development because it forced me to recognise that technical difficulty and responsible use are separate questions.</p>
        <p>I researched how the services exposed media URLs and built an interface that accepted a song-page link from QQ Music, NetEase Cloud Music, KuGou, or KuWo. It could download individual tracks at different quality levels and process multiple pages from an artist catalogue. I also built an account system and restricted some functions to selected users. Technically, it was the most complex application I had attempted at that point; ethically, it was a project I should not have released.</p>
        <p>I closed the service after about six months. By then, more than four hundred accounts had been created and the system had processed thousands of downloads. I no longer treat those numbers as a measure of success. What matters in retrospect is that the project changed how I think about permission, platform rules, and the responsibility that comes with making software available to other people.</p>
