<template>
  <div>
    <h1 ref="researchTop" class="research-title" data-scroll-reveal>
      Research I've Been Part Of
    </h1>
    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p>My research connects three questions. How do people form representations that support memory, generalisation, and transfer? What internal structures and computations produce the behaviour of AI models? And when people work with AI, how does the tool change what they attend to, practise, understand, and decide?</p>
      <transition name="research-details">
        <div v-if="introExpanded" class="research-details">
          <p>I study human-AI interaction as a cognitive and design problem. As AI enters learning, writing, work, and decision-making, ability becomes distributed between the person and the tool. I want to understand when that distribution helps people think more effectively, when it weakens their command of the task, and how systems can preserve agency, judgment, and understanding rather than merely increasing speed.</p>
          <p>On the human side, I study how learners move from examples to reusable structure. Behaviour alone often cannot reveal whether someone learned a rule, memorised particular items, or recombined familiar components. I use experiments and computational models to ask which representations could have produced the observed pattern, how those representations change with experience, and what a proposed experiment can actually distinguish. Some of this work is conducted as a part-time research assistant at HKU's <a href="https://slrlab.edu.hku.hk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SLR Lab</a>, with Dr. Stephen M. K. Lee.</p>
          <p>On the AI side, I study the mechanisms behind model behaviour. A correct answer does not by itself show how the model represented the problem or which internal components produced the result. I use behavioural tests and causal interventions to examine what models encode, how those representations are used, where competing processes cancel or override one another, and whether an apparent capability rests on a stable mechanism.</p>
          <p>The long-term aim is to let the study of minds and models inform one another. Cognitive models can sharpen the questions we ask about AI mechanisms; AI systems can provide new tools and comparison cases for studying human cognition; and work on their interaction can guide tools that help people learn, reason, create, and decide with greater agency.</p>
        </div>
      </transition>
      <button
