<template>
  <div class="project-story search-story">
    <p class="back-link"><router-link :to="{ path: '/projects', query: { to: 'searchstory' } }">&larr; Back to projects</router-link></p>
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>How This Search Was Built</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        This static site has no conventional search backend, but it supports both semantic search and grounded
        question answering. Search is prepared at build time and runs in the browser. Ask uses the same site content
        to assemble context, then sends the prompt to a small language model running on an edge worker. The
        demonstrations below use the live index and production code.
      </p>

      <section class="story-hero" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">The site laid out by meaning</span>
          <h2>A map of everything written here</h2>
          <p>
            During the build, each passage is converted into a 384-dimensional embedding. UMAP projects those
            vectors onto a two-dimensional plane, placing passages with similar meanings near one another. Research
            entries form one region, travel notes another, while fragments occupy several overlapping areas. A query
            is embedded with the same model and added to the map, so its position shows which passages are
            semantically closest.
          </p>
          <p>
            The nearest-passage list in this section uses semantic similarity alone. The site's main search combines
            that signal with exact word matching. The next demonstration separates the two rankings and shows how
            they are merged.
          </p>
        </div>
        <EmbeddingMap />
      </section>

      <h2>Two rankers, one query</h2>
      <p>
        The lexical ranker rewards rare words shared by the query and a passage. It is strong when someone enters an
        exact name such as “HSBC” or “Lexaday,” but it cannot recognise a paraphrase that uses different vocabulary.
        The semantic ranker compares embeddings, so a query about “learning vocabulary every day” can still retrieve
        Lexaday. It is less reliable for short names and literal identifiers. The final search uses weighted
        reciprocal-rank fusion to combine both lists, while protecting the strongest exact matches from being buried.
        The three columns below show the lexical, semantic, and fused results for the same query.
      </p>

      <div class="lab" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <form class="lab-form index-ignore" @submit.prevent="runLab">
          <input
            v-model="labQuery"
            class="lab-input"
            type="text"
            autocomplete="off"
            spellcheck="false"
            placeholder="Ask the index something"
            aria-label="Query for the ranking demo"
          >
          <button type="submit" class="lab-go" :disabled="labBusy">{{ labBusy ? '…' : 'Rank' }}</button>
        </form>
        <div class="lab-samples index-ignore">
          <button v-for="s in labSamples" :key="s" type="button" class="lab-chip" :disabled="labBusy" @click="labQuery = s; runLab();">{{ s }}</button>
        </div>
        <p v-if="labStatus" class="lab-status">{{ labStatus }}</p>

        <div v-if="fusedTop.length" class="lab-columns">
          <div class="lab-col">
            <p class="lab-col-title">Lexical</p>
            <p class="lab-col-sub">rare shared words</p>
            <ol>
              <li v-for="r in lexTop" :key="`l${r.id}`">
                <span class="lab-hit-title">{{ r.title }}</span>
                <span class="lab-bar"><span class="lab-bar-fill" :style="{ width: r.pct + '%' }"></span></span>
              </li>
              <li v-if="!lexTop.length" class="lab-none">No word overlap at all.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="lab-col">
            <p class="lab-col-title">Semantic</p>
            <p class="lab-col-sub">cosine similarity</p>
            <ol>
              <li v-for="r in semTop" :key="`s${r.id}`">
                <span class="lab-hit-title">{{ r.title }}</span>
                <span class="lab-bar"><span class="lab-bar-fill" :style="{ width: r.pct + '%' }"></span></span>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="lab-col lab-col-fused">
            <p class="lab-col-title">Fused</p>
            <p class="lab-col-sub">what the palette shows</p>
            <ol>
              <li v-for="r in fusedTop" :key="`f${r.id}`">
                <span class="lab-hit-title">{{ r.title }}</span>
                <span class="lab-badges">{{ r.badge }}</span>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Drawing the line</h2>
      <p>
        Search also needs to decide when no result is relevant enough to show. Every query receives a semantic score
        against all {{ corpusSize || 152 }} passages, so the system needs a threshold separating weak relevance from
        unrelated content. I tuned that threshold on a small set of realistic queries and deliberately unrelated
        queries. The unrelated examples remained at or below about 0.22, most genuine matches were above 0.45, and a
        few useful matches fell near 0.26. I set the production floor to 0.24 to favour recall while still rejecting
        clearly unrelated requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also tested e5-small, but its scores were compressed into a much narrower range, making a fixed threshold
        less reliable without improving recall enough to justify the trade-off. Run a query above, then move the
        line to see which passages remain.
      </p>

      <div v-if="scores.length" class="floor-demo" data-scroll-pop="true">
        <div class="floor-strip" aria-hidden="true">
          <span
            v-for="(s, i) in scores"
            :key="i"
            class="floor-dot"
            :class="{ pass: s >= floor }"
            :style="{ left: scoreX(s) + '%' }"
          ></span>
          <span class="floor-line" :style="{ left: scoreX(floor) + '%' }"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="floor-controls index-ignore">
          <input v-model.number="floor" type="range" min="0" max="0.6" step="0.005" aria-label="Score floor">
          <p class="floor-readout">
            floor {{ floor.toFixed(3) }} · <strong>{{ passCount }}</strong> of {{ scores.length }} passages
            survive<span v-if="Math.abs(floor - defaultFloor) > 0.002"> (the site ships with {{ defaultFloor }})</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p v-else class="floor-empty">Run a query in the lab above and the score distribution appears here.</p>

      <h2>From search to answers</h2>
      <p>
        Ask builds on the same corpus, but its job is different from search. The site's fixed pages are small enough
        to include together, while the ranking is used mainly to select which fragment entries join the prompt. A
        Cloudflare Worker generates the answer and falls back across a set of free-tier models if one is
        unavailable. A parallel EdgeOne function serves visitors in mainland China. The prompt restricts the model to
        the supplied passages and asks it to state clearly when the site does not contain the requested information.
      </p>

      <div class="process-strip" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="120">
        <div class="process-step">
          <span class="step-num">1</span>
          <h3>Gather</h3>
          <p>Include the fixed site pages and use the ranking to select the most relevant fragment entries.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span class="step-num">2</span>
          <h3>Prompt</h3>
          <p>Number the passages and place them in a grounded prompt that prohibits unsupported claims and requests source citations.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span class="step-num">3</span>
          <h3>Generate</h3>
          <p>Send the prompt to an edge worker, with a fallback chain if the preferred free-tier model is unavailable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span class="step-num">4</span>
          <h3>Cite</h3>
          <p>Return bracketed source numbers with the answer and display only the pages that were actually used.</p>
